From 380265c6cc89402010e28823ab075bca6f8e9549 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 09:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] rzv2n-dev: ALP E1M DEEPX 0.75V rail bring-up before PCIe

The V2N-M1 SoM's DEEPX DX-M1 core rail (DA9292 CH2, 0.75V) is currently
not sequenced by any code path -- patch 0001 releases M1_RESET and
enables the PCIe mux purely on the on-module EEPROM manifest match, with
no rail step in front of it.  The DA9292-AROVx OTP variant this SoM uses
boots with CH2_VSTEP=1 (doubled voltage range, PMC_CTRL_01 reset 0x80):
releasing M1_RESET before CH2 is confirmed at 0.75V and power-good risks
an over-voltage event on the DEEPX core rail the instant the chip starts
drawing current, not merely a brownout.

board_late_init() now runs alp_deepx_rail_bringup() BEFORE
alp_deepx_pcie_bringup(), gated on the new CONFIG_ALP_E1M_DEEPX_RAIL (set
via meta-alp-sdk's deepx-rail.cfg for the V2M MACHINEs only, default n
otherwise) OR the same alp_som_is_v2n_m1() EEPROM check patch 0001 already
uses -- either is enough to attempt the rail, so a V2M-family image still
sequences it even before its manifest is burned, and any V2N-family image
still sequences it on physical V2N-M1 hardware without needing the
Kconfig.  alp_deepx_pcie_bringup() keeps its own alp_som_is_v2n_m1() gate
unchanged and additionally requires the rail step to have succeeded --
M1_RESET is never released with the rail down.

Sequence, over DM I2C bus 8 (RIIC8 / BRD_I2C) at 7-bit address 0x1E:

  Program phase (unconditional; every write read back; any mismatch
  aborts without ever touching CH2_EN):
    1. PMC_DEV_ID (0x19) must read the exact OTP variant (0xEA).
    2. PMC_STATUS_01 (0x01) must read 0x00 (no thermal/UVLO latched).
    3. If PMC_CTRL_01 (0x07) has CH2_VSTEP (bit7) or CH2_EN (bit1) set,
       clear both together with a read-modify-write (never a constant
       write -- preserves CH1_EN and everything else), then read back.
    4. Program both CH2 VSEL registers (0x0C, 0x0D) to 0x96 (0.75V at
       VSTEP=0) -- VSEL2 pin routing is undocumented on this SoM
       (da9292.yaml EN2/VSEL2 v2n_route: TBD), so both setpoints get
       written defensively.
    5. Read back 0x0C/0x0D; confirm CH1's VOUT (0x0A) and CTRL_01 bit0
       (CH1_EN) are untouched.
    6. Read + write-1-to-clear PMC_EVENT_00/01 so a stale fault can't
       false-trip the enable-phase poll below.
    Idempotent on a warm reboot: if CTRL_01 already has VSTEP=0 and
    both VOUT_CH2 registers already read 0x96, steps 3-5's writes are
    skipped entirely -- CH2_EN may legitimately already be 1 with the
    DEEPX drawing power off it, and clearing it would drop a rail that
    is already correctly up.

  Enable phase (only after the program phase passes):
    P65 (DEEPX_PWR_EN_REQ) is polled as an input for up to 500ms; if it
    never goes high, P64 stays low, M1_RESET (PA6) is driven low, and
    the function returns false without ever writing CH2_EN.  Otherwise:
    7. Set CH2_EN in PMC_CTRL_01.
    8. Poll PMC_STATUS_00 up to 20ms for CH2_PG=1 with CH2_UV/OV/OC all
       clear, and PMC_EVENT_00 free of a latched CH2_OV/OC.
    9. On failure, immediately clear CH2_EN, drive P64 and PA6 low, and
       return false.
    10. On success, drive P64 (DEEPX_CORE_0P75_EN) high, wait 5ms, and
        re-read STATUS_00 to confirm PG held with no UV before handing
        off to alp_deepx_pcie_bringup().

Adds two small GPIO helpers (alp_gpio_input_configure /
alp_gpio_input_get) alongside patch 0001's alp_gpio_output -- same
P/PM/PMC register block, PM=0x1 selects input mode instead of 0x2; the
live pin level is read from the separate PFC terminal-input register
(PFC_BASE+0x0820+n), not from P -- see below for why that distinction
matters.

RIIC8 pinmux (P06/P07) and the i2c8 devicetree node + alias are already
enabled in this tree (both predate patch 0001, confirmed unchanged in
its diff context) -- no further devicetree wiring is needed here.

This is the ONLY code, on any core, that writes the DA9292 or drives
P64: the CM33/Zephyr side has no equivalent -- src/zephyr/v2n_power_mgmt.c
does not exist, and metadata/pinmux/v2n.yaml attributes P64/P65 to core
"a55" so a CM33 board file cannot claim them.

alp_gpio_input_get() reads the P output-latch register (PFC_BASE+0x0020+n,
last value written), not the live pin level. Adds ALP_PFC_PIN(n) =
PFC_BASE+0x0820+n (the dedicated terminal input register; TF-A
pfc_regs.h PFC_PIN2A=+0x082A confirms the offset, bench-read
PIN6=0x10410826 confirmed a real input bit) and switches the read to
it; removes the comment that wrongly claimed no separate PIN register
exists on this family.

Every program-phase abort (DA9292 reachable onward) now forces P64 low
/ PA6 (M1_RESET) low via a new alp_deepx_rail_safe_off() helper before
returning, not only the enable-phase failures that already did this
inline; the three existing inline pairs converge onto the same helper.

Hardening: immediately before the CH2_EN write, the CTRL_01 value just
re-read for that write is re-checked for CH2_VSTEP==0 and the rail is
aborted via the same helper if it is not -- the exact over-voltage
condition this whole sequence exists to prevent.

The PG-timeout failure message now also hints that CH2 may require
EN2/P64 high before power-good asserts (see docs/bring-up-v2n-m1.md
Sec 2), alongside the existing STATUS_00/EVENT_00 dump. The enable
sequence itself (CH2_EN over I2C, poll PG, then P64) is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Alp Lab AB <contact@alplab.ai>
---
 board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/Kconfig     |  25 +++
 board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c | 330 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 354 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/Kconfig b/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/Kconfig
index 7a9885f7..f1807399 100644
--- a/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/Kconfig
+++ b/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/Kconfig
@@ -74,3 +74,28 @@ config ALP_E1M_FDTFILE
 	  Set the basename only.  KERNEL_DEVICETREE carries a "renesas/"
 	  prefix that the deploy step strips, so the file lands at
 	  boot/<basename> on the target.
+
+config ALP_E1M_DEEPX_RAIL
+	bool "ALP E1M: sequence the on-module DA9292 DEEPX 0.75V rail before PCIe bring-up"
+	depends on TARGET_RZV2N_DEV || TARGET_RZV2N_EVK
+	default n
+	help
+	  Force board_late_init() to run alp_deepx_rail_bringup() even
+	  before the on-module EEPROM manifest is checked --
+	  alp_som_is_v2n_m1() alone already triggers it on any board whose
+	  manifest reports family "v2n-m1", so this matters only for a
+	  V2M-family image built before its EEPROM is burned.
+
+	  Sequences the DA9292 PMIC's CH2 to 0.75V and polls it to
+	  power-good before alp_deepx_pcie_bringup() is allowed to release
+	  M1_RESET (PA6) -- releasing the DX-M1 reset with the rail down,
+	  or worse still at its OTP-default doubled voltage range, risks
+	  an over-voltage event on the DEEPX core rail.  U-Boot is the
+	  sole writer of the DA9292 and the sole driver of P64
+	  (DEEPX_CORE_0P75_EN) / P65 (DEEPX_PWR_EN_REQ) -- the CM33 must
+	  never master BRD_I2C or claim these pads (see
+	  metadata/pinmux/v2n.yaml and
+	  metadata/e1m_modules/v2n/core-ownership.yaml in alp-sdk).
+
+	  Set via meta-alp-sdk's deepx-rail.cfg for the V2M MACHINEs only;
+	  leave off (default) for the V2N base build.
diff --git a/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c b/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c
index a20ccae8..689c3062 100644
--- a/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c
+++ b/board/renesas/rzv2n-dev/rzv2n-dev.c
@@ -284,6 +284,7 @@ static void board_usb_init(void)
 #define ALP_PFC_P(n)		((void *)(uintptr_t)(PFC_BASE + 0x0020 + (n)))		/* data, 8-bit */
 #define ALP_PFC_PM(n)		((void *)(uintptr_t)(PFC_BASE + 0x0140 + (n) * 2))	/* mode, 16-bit */
 #define ALP_PFC_PMC(n)		((void *)(uintptr_t)(PFC_BASE + 0x0220 + (n)))		/* mode ctrl, 8-bit */
+#define ALP_PFC_PIN(n)		((void *)(uintptr_t)(PFC_BASE + 0x0820 + (n)))		/* terminal input, 8-bit */
 #define ALP_PM_OUTPUT		0x2
 
 static void alp_gpio_output(unsigned int bank, unsigned int pin, int value)
@@ -360,9 +361,336 @@ static void alp_deepx_pcie_bringup(void)
 	printf("ALP: DEEPX DX-M1 PCIe path enabled (mux on, reset released)\n");
 }
 
+/*
+ * ALP V2M (E1M-V2M1xx): sequence the on-module DA9292 PMIC's CH2 rail to
+ * 0.75V and enable it BEFORE alp_deepx_pcie_bringup() ever releases
+ * M1_RESET.  This matters because the DA9292-AROVx OTP variant on this
+ * SoM boots with CH2_VSTEP=1 (doubled voltage range): releasing the DX-M1
+ * reset with CH2 still at its OTP default risks an over-voltage event on
+ * the DEEPX core rail the instant the chip starts drawing current, not
+ * just an under-voltage brownout.  U-Boot is the SOLE writer of this PMIC
+ * and the sole driver of P64 (DEEPX_CORE_0P75_EN); the CM33 must never
+ * master BRD_I2C or claim P64/P65 -- see
+ * metadata/pinmux/v2n.yaml (core: "a55" on both pads) and
+ * metadata/e1m_modules/v2n/core-ownership.yaml.
+ *
+ * Gated on CONFIG_ALP_E1M_DEEPX_RAIL (set via deepx-rail.cfg on V2M
+ * MACHINEs only) OR alp_som_is_v2n_m1() -- either is sufficient to run
+ * THIS function, so a V2M image still sequences the rail even before its
+ * EEPROM manifest is burned, and a V2N-family image still sequences the
+ * rail on physical V2N-M1 hardware even without the Kconfig.
+ * alp_deepx_pcie_bringup() keeps its OWN alp_som_is_v2n_m1() gate
+ * unchanged -- M1_RESET is only ever released on confirmed V2N-M1
+ * hardware, never merely because the Kconfig forced a rail attempt.
+ *
+ * Returns true only when CH2 is confirmed power-good (STATUS_00 CH2_PG=1,
+ * no UV/OV/OC) and P64 has been driven high.  Any other return means the
+ * caller must NOT run alp_deepx_pcie_bringup() -- the mux/reset stay down
+ * with the rail.
+ */
+
+#define ALP_DA9292_I2C_BUS  8		/* DM I2C bus number for RIIC8 / BRD_I2C */
+#define ALP_DA9292_I2C_ADDR 0x1E	/* 7-bit; DA9292-AROVx OTP variant, see da9292.h */
+
+/* PMC_* register offsets + bit positions -- Renesas DA9292 Datasheet
+ * Rev 2.2 (R16DS0518EJ0220) Tables 12-27; mirrors the register map already
+ * verified in alp-sdk's chips/da9292/da9292.c (this file cannot include
+ * that header -- U-Boot builds standalone against upstream sources only). */
+#define DA9292_REG_STATUS_00   0x00
+#define DA9292_REG_STATUS_01   0x01
+#define DA9292_REG_EVENT_00    0x02
+#define DA9292_REG_EVENT_01    0x03
+#define DA9292_REG_CTRL_01     0x07
+#define DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH1_00 0x0A
+#define DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH2_00 0x0C
+#define DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH2_01 0x0D
+#define DA9292_REG_DEV_ID      0x19
+#define DA9292_REG_REV_ID      0x1A
+#define DA9292_REG_CFG_REV     0x1B
+
+#define DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_VSTEP BIT(7)
+#define DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_EN    BIT(1)
+#define DA9292_CTRL01_CH1_EN    BIT(0)
+
+#define DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_OC BIT(7)
+#define DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_OV BIT(5)
+#define DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_UV BIT(3)
+#define DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_PG BIT(1)
+
+#define DA9292_EVENT00_CH2_OC BIT(7)
+#define DA9292_EVENT00_CH2_OV BIT(5)
+
+#define DA9292_DEV_ID_EXPECTED 0xEA	/* DA9292-AROVx OTP variant on this SoM */
+#define DA9292_VOUT_750MV_BYTE 0x96	/* VSTEP=0 (5 mV/step) encoding of 0.75 V */
+
+static int alp_da9292_reg_read(struct udevice *dev, u8 reg, u8 *val)
+{
+	return dm_i2c_read(dev, reg, val, 1);
+}
+
+static int alp_da9292_reg_write(struct udevice *dev, u8 reg, u8 val)
+{
+	return dm_i2c_write(dev, reg, &val, 1);
+}
+
+/* Renesas RZ/V2N PFC input read-back: reads the dedicated PFC_PINxx terminal
+ * input register (PFC_BASE+0x0820+n), NOT the P output-latch register --
+ * P only reflects what was last WRITTEN, not the live pin level
+ * (bench-confirmed: PIN6=0x10410826 read bit5=1 with PM6=0x0400/input). */
+#define ALP_PM_INPUT 0x1
+
+static void alp_gpio_input_configure(unsigned int bank, unsigned int pin)
+{
+	u16 pm;
+
+	clrbits_8(ALP_PFC_PMC(bank), BIT(pin));		/* GPIO mode */
+
+	pm = readw(ALP_PFC_PM(bank));
+	pm &= ~(0x3 << (pin * 2));
+	pm |= (ALP_PM_INPUT << (pin * 2));
+	writew(pm, ALP_PFC_PM(bank));
+}
+
+static int alp_gpio_input_get(unsigned int bank, unsigned int pin)
+{
+	return !!(readb(ALP_PFC_PIN(bank)) & BIT(pin));
+}
+
+/* Force the DEEPX rail path to a known-safe state (P64 low,
+ * M1_RESET asserted) on ANY abort from the point the DA9292 is confirmed
+ * reachable onward -- not just the enable-phase failures that already did
+ * this inline. Cheap and idempotent even when neither pin has been driven
+ * yet this boot; the point is no abort path can ever leave P64 high with
+ * an unconfirmed or dropped rail. */
+static void alp_deepx_rail_safe_off(void)
+{
+	alp_gpio_output(6, 4, 0);	/* P64 low */
+	alp_gpio_output(10, 6, 0);	/* PA6 M1_RESET asserted */
+}
+
+static bool alp_deepx_rail_bringup(void)
+{
+	struct udevice *dev;
+	u8 dev_id = 0, rev_id = 0, cfg_rev = 0;
+	u8 status00 = 0, status01 = 0;
+	u8 ctrl01 = 0, ctrl01_en;
+	u8 vout_ch2_00 = 0, vout_ch2_01 = 0;
+	u8 vout_ch1_before = 0, vout_ch1_after = 0;
+	u8 event00 = 0, event01 = 0;
+	bool already_programmed;
+	int ret, level, waited_ms;
+	unsigned int t;
+
+	ret = i2c_get_chip_for_busnum(ALP_DA9292_I2C_BUS, ALP_DA9292_I2C_ADDR, 1, &dev);
+	if (ret) {
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 not reachable on RIIC8/BRD_I2C (%d)\n", ret);
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	/* 1. Identity -- abort on anything but the exact OTP variant. */
+	ret = alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_DEV_ID, &dev_id);
+	if (ret || dev_id != DA9292_DEV_ID_EXPECTED) {
+		alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_REV_ID, &rev_id);
+		alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_CFG_REV, &cfg_rev);
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 DEV_ID=0x%02x (want 0x%02x) REV_ID=0x%02x CFG_REV=0x%02x ret=%d -- abort\n",
+		       dev_id, DA9292_DEV_ID_EXPECTED, rev_id, cfg_rev, ret);
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	/* 2. No thermal/UVLO status latched on entry. */
+	ret = alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_STATUS_01, &status01);
+	if (ret || status01 != 0x00) {
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 STATUS_01=0x%02x ret=%d (want 0x00) -- abort\n", status01, ret);
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	ret = alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, &ctrl01);
+	ret |= alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH2_00, &vout_ch2_00);
+	ret |= alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH2_01, &vout_ch2_01);
+	ret |= alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH1_00, &vout_ch1_before);
+	if (ret) {
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 pre-program register read failed\n");
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	/* Idempotent warm-reboot path: CH2 already at 0.75V/VSTEP=0 means
+	 * CH2_EN may already be 1 with the DEEPX actively drawing power off
+	 * it -- clearing CH2_EN here (step 3 below) would drop a rail that
+	 * is already correctly up. */
+	already_programmed = !(ctrl01 & DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_VSTEP) &&
+			      vout_ch2_00 == DA9292_VOUT_750MV_BYTE &&
+			      vout_ch2_01 == DA9292_VOUT_750MV_BYTE;
+
+	if (already_programmed) {
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 CH2 already 0.75V/VSTEP=0 (warm reboot) -- skipping program phase\n");
+	} else {
+		/* 3. Clear VSTEP + EN together, NEVER a constant write. */
+		if (ctrl01 & (DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_VSTEP | DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_EN)) {
+			u8 want = ctrl01 & (u8)~(DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_VSTEP | DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_EN);
+			u8 got = 0;
+
+			ret = alp_da9292_reg_write(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, want);
+			ret |= alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, &got);
+			if (ret || got != want) {
+				printf("ALP: DA9292 CTRL_01 clear wrote 0x%02x read 0x%02x ret=%d -- abort\n",
+				       want, got, ret);
+				alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+				return false;
+			}
+			ctrl01 = got;
+		}
+
+		/* 4. Program both VSEL setpoints -- VSEL2 pin routing is
+		 * unknown (da9292.yaml EN2/VSEL2 v2n_route: TBD), so both
+		 * CH2 registers get the same 0.75V byte defensively. */
+		ret = alp_da9292_reg_write(dev, DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH2_00, DA9292_VOUT_750MV_BYTE);
+		ret |= alp_da9292_reg_write(dev, DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH2_01, DA9292_VOUT_750MV_BYTE);
+		if (ret) {
+			printf("ALP: DA9292 VOUT_CH2 write failed (%d) -- abort\n", ret);
+			alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+			return false;
+		}
+
+		/* 5. Read back. */
+		ret = alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH2_00, &vout_ch2_00);
+		ret |= alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH2_01, &vout_ch2_01);
+		if (ret || vout_ch2_00 != DA9292_VOUT_750MV_BYTE || vout_ch2_01 != DA9292_VOUT_750MV_BYTE) {
+			printf("ALP: DA9292 VOUT_CH2 readback 0x%02x/0x%02x ret=%d (want 0x%02x/0x%02x) -- abort\n",
+			       vout_ch2_00, vout_ch2_01, ret, DA9292_VOUT_750MV_BYTE, DA9292_VOUT_750MV_BYTE);
+			alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+			return false;
+		}
+	}
+
+	/* 5 (both paths): CH1 must be untouched and still enabled. */
+	ret = alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_VOUT_CH1_00, &vout_ch1_after);
+	ret |= alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, &ctrl01);
+	if (ret || vout_ch1_after != vout_ch1_before || !(ctrl01 & DA9292_CTRL01_CH1_EN)) {
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 CH1 disturbed VOUT_CH1_00 0x%02x->0x%02x CTRL_01=0x%02x ret=%d -- abort\n",
+		       vout_ch1_before, vout_ch1_after, ctrl01, ret);
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	/* 6. Clear any latched events so a stale fault can't false-trip the
+	 * enable-phase poll below. */
+	ret = alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_EVENT_00, &event00);
+	ret |= alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_EVENT_01, &event01);
+	if (ret) {
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 EVENT read failed (%d) -- abort\n", ret);
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		return false;
+	}
+	if (event00)
+		alp_da9292_reg_write(dev, DA9292_REG_EVENT_00, event00);
+	if (event01)
+		alp_da9292_reg_write(dev, DA9292_REG_EVENT_01, event01);
+
+	printf("ALP: DA9292 programmed CTRL_01=0x%02x VOUT_CH2=0x%02x/0x%02x\n",
+	       ctrl01, vout_ch2_00, vout_ch2_01);
+
+	/* Enable phase: only if the A55 asked for it via P65
+	 * (DEEPX_PWR_EN_REQ), polled up to 500 ms. */
+	alp_gpio_input_configure(6, 5);
+	level = 0;
+	for (waited_ms = 0; waited_ms <= 500; waited_ms++) {
+		level = alp_gpio_input_get(6, 5);
+		if (level)
+			break;
+		mdelay(1);
+	}
+	if (!level) {
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		printf("ALP: DEEPX_PWR_EN_REQ (P65) low -- DEEPX rail not enabled\n");
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	/* 7. CH2_EN = 1. */
+	ret = alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, &ctrl01);
+	if (ret) {
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 CTRL_01 pre-enable read failed (%d) -- abort\n", ret);
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	/* Re-assert on the FRESH CTRL_01 read above that
+	 * CH2_VSTEP is still 0 immediately before setting CH2_EN -- a
+	 * VSTEP=1 CH2_EN would run CH2 at double range, the exact
+	 * over-voltage this whole rail sequence exists to prevent. */
+	if (ctrl01 & DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_VSTEP) {
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 CTRL_01=0x%02x has CH2_VSTEP set at enable time -- abort\n", ctrl01);
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		if (ctrl01 & DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_EN)
+			alp_da9292_reg_write(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, (u8)(ctrl01 & ~DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_EN));
+		return false;
+	}
+	ctrl01_en = ctrl01 | DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_EN;
+	ret = alp_da9292_reg_write(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, ctrl01_en);
+	if (ret) {
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 CH2_EN write failed (%d) -- abort\n", ret);
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	/* 8. Poll up to 20 ms for CH2 PG with no UV/OV/OC, plus no latched
+	 * CH2 OV/OC event. */
+	for (t = 0; t <= 20; t++) {
+		ret = alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_STATUS_00, &status00);
+		if (!ret && (status00 & DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_PG) &&
+		    !(status00 & (DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_UV | DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_OV | DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_OC)))
+			break;
+		mdelay(1);
+	}
+	event00 = 0;
+	alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_EVENT_00, &event00);
+	if (!(status00 & DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_PG) ||
+	    (status00 & (DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_UV | DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_OV | DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_OC)) ||
+	    (event00 & (DA9292_EVENT00_CH2_OV | DA9292_EVENT00_CH2_OC))) {
+		/* 9. Disable immediately -- never leave CH2_EN set on a
+		 * failed enable. */
+		alp_da9292_reg_write(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, (u8)(ctrl01_en & ~DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_EN));
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 CH2 enable failed STATUS_00=0x%02x EVENT_00=0x%02x -- DEEPX rail not enabled"
+		       " (CH2 may require EN2/P64 high before PG -- see bring-up doc)\n",
+		       status00, event00);
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	/* 10. Success: drive P64 high, settle, re-verify before handing off. */
+	alp_gpio_output(6, 4, 1);
+	mdelay(5);
+	ret = alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_STATUS_00, &status00);
+	if (ret || !(status00 & DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_PG) || (status00 & DA9292_STATUS00_CH2_UV)) {
+		/* P64/PA6 go low FIRST, unconditionally, before
+		 * touching CTRL_01 -- so the pin state is safe regardless of EN2
+		 * pin semantics. The CTRL_01 clear is still issued and a second
+		 * safe_off() follows it (idempotent) so it is the last word. */
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		alp_da9292_reg_read(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, &ctrl01);
+		alp_da9292_reg_write(dev, DA9292_REG_CTRL_01, (u8)(ctrl01 & ~DA9292_CTRL01_CH2_EN));
+		alp_deepx_rail_safe_off();
+		printf("ALP: DA9292 CH2 dropped after P64 high STATUS_00=0x%02x ret=%d -- DEEPX rail disabled\n",
+		       status00, ret);
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	printf("ALP: DEEPX rail 0.75V up (PG)\n");
+	return true;
+}
